Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and converts it to an HTML formatted string:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <omanomanip>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string htmlTxt; 
    std::string htmlTxt = "<html><body><pre>" + markdown;
    htmlTxt += "</pre></body></html>";
    return htmlTxt;
}
```
This function uses basic string manipulation to insert the markdown formatted text within HTML tags. It initialize the `std::string` variable with `<html><body><pre>`. It then concaten the markdown formatted text into the variable and appends `<pre></body></html>`. Finally, it returns the constructed HTML string.

Here's a sample usage of our function:
```cpp
int main(){
   std::string markdown = "Hello, Markdown!";
   std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
   std::cout << html << std::endl;
   return 0;
}
```
Above, our `main()` function initializes a `std::string` called `markdown` with the value "Hello, Markdown!", calls the `markdownToHtml()` function to convert it to HTML formatted string, then prints it to the console.

